<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · ideology</p><h1 class="enc-title">Christian democracy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1891 CE – present</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Christian democracy is an ideology inspired by Christian social teaching, drawn mainly from Catholic social thought and neo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calvinism">Calvinism</a>. Christian democrats led postwar Western and Southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in building welfare states and constructing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a>, and their parties remain influential in Europe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His government, which Orbán himself called an illiberal <b>Christian democracy</b>, was variously described as a hybrid regime or dominant-party system, and the European Union moved to penalise Hungary over the rule of law.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hungary">Hungary · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
